## DESCRIPTION
## Calculus Two, Sequences
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Infinite sequences and series)
## DBsection(Limit of a sequence)
## Date(09/16/2018)
## Institution(Community College of Denver, Colorado Community College System)
## Author(Brittni Lorton)
## KEYWORDS('calculus', 'sequences', 'identify bounded', 'identify monotonicity')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "parserPopUp.pl", "PGcourse.pl",);

Context('Numeric')->variables->are(n => "Real");
$a      = random(2, 6);
$popup1 = DropDown([ "is", "is not" ], "is not");
$popup2 =
    DropDown([ "monotone increasing", "monotone decreasing", "not monotone" ],
    "not monotone");
$popup3 = DropDown([ "is", "is not" ], "is");
$popup4 =
    DropDown([ "monotone increasing", "monotone decreasing", "not monotone" ],
        "monotone increasing");

BEGIN_PGML
For the sequences [``\{a_n\} = \{[$a]\tan(n)\}``] and [``\{b_n\} = \{[$a]\tan^{-1}(n)\}``], determine whether the sequence is bounded and whether it is eventually monotone increasing, monotone decreasing, or not monotone. 

a) [``\{a_n\} = \{[$a]\tan(n)\}``] [_]{$popup1} bounded.

b) [``\{a_n\} = \{[$a]\tan(n)\}``] is eventually  [_]{$popup2}.

c) [``\{b_n\} = \{[$a]\tan^{-1}(n)\}``] [_]{$popup3} bounded.

d) [``\{b_n\} = \{[$a]\tan^{-1}(n)\}``] is eventually  [___]{$popup4}.
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) Because [`\tan x`] isn't bounded, then the sequence is not either.
b) [`\tan x`] is periodic, so isn't monotone. 
c) [`\tan^{-1} x`] is bounded--it's always in [` (\pi/2,\pi/2)`] so the sequence is as well.
d) The function [`\tan^{-1} x`] is always increasing.  Recall that it's derivative is 
    [``` \frac{d}{dx} \tan^{-1} x = \frac{1}{1+x^2} ```]
    which is always positive.  Therefore the sequence is monotonic increasing as well.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
